<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Orchha State</h1><p class="st-meta">1531 CE – 1950 CE · capital Tikamgarh</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Bundela Rajput kingdom of Bundelkhand, whose riverside capital of palaces and temples was built by Bir Singh Deo, an ally of the emperor Jahangir.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1531 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bundelas">Bundela</a> chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudra-pratap-singh">Rudra Pratap Singh</a> founded Orchha on an island in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/betwa-river">Betwa</a> river and made it his capital. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bundela">Bundela Rajputs</a> had risen in the region after the decline of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chandela-dynasty">Chandela dynasty</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Orchha's rulers resisted and then served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vir-singh-deo">Bir Singh Deo</a> had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar's</a> minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abul-fazl-ibn-mubarak">Abul Fazl</a> killed in 1602 on behalf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahangir">Prince Salim</a>, the future Jahangir, and was rewarded with power and wealth; he built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahangir-mahal">Jahangir Mahal</a> and many temples. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ram-raja-temple">Ram Raja temple</a> made Orchha a pilgrimage centre. In 1783 the capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tikamgarh">Tikamgarh</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Orchha signed a treaty with the British in 1812. In 1950 it joined <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/vindhya-pradesh">Vindhya Pradesh</a>, which merged into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a> in 1956. The old capital's palaces and cenotaphs are major monuments.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/orchha-state.jpg" alt="Orchha State" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Germenfer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Orchha_State.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1531 CE – 1950 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tikamgarh</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Rudra Pratap Singh, Bir Singh Deo</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bundeli</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>India</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Orchha, Tikamgarh State</dd></dl></aside></div>
